{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y1YZ6nojmpfKArLr1xUSpm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019Y4SNxV87Wbg6K9nDQW73J","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Step 2: Cross-Share — Findings from R2 (Novelty & Related Work)\n\nR2 has identified the following issues relevant to your methods/experiments assessment:\n\n1. **Missing references (critical):** Multiple \"?\" and \"???\" citation placeholders throughout §1 and §2. The References section (page 10) is completely empty. This is a submission-readiness issue that also prevents verifying related work claims.\n\n2. **No figures in the entire paper.** Zero visual elements across 9 pages. Power curves, forest plots, and heatmaps would significantly strengthen the experimental narrative. The experiment plan apparently included detailed figure specifications that are absent.\n\n3. **Title vs. content tension:** \"The Likert Blindspot\" frames the finding as a categorical failure, but by §4.6, it's characterized as an \"efficiency gap.\" R2 flags this as potential overselling.\n\n4. **Incremental novelty concern:** The Likert-vs-forced-choice gap is well-established in psychometrics (Kiritchenko & Mohammad, Best-Worst Scaling). The paper's contribution is applying this known insight to LLM judge bias. R2 questions whether this is sufficient for a top venue.\n\n5. **Scope concern:** Title says \"Style Bias\" but only formality is tested. The gap between title promise and experimental scope is notable.\n\nPlease factor these observations into your final assessment, especially: (a) whether the statistical evidence would be strengthened by figures, (b) whether the \"efficiency gap\" recharacterization at n=500 undermines the \"blindspot\" framing, and (c) whether the narrow scope (1 clean cell after confound analysis) is sufficient for the claims made.","reviewer_id":"1","project":"causal_style_decomp_judge"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2767,"cache_read_input_tokens":62952,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2767},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a33c06a6-9d6e-466c-b93b-d82fa976056a","uuid":"89d43c61-e42f-4273-a607-20a47fabc464"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019Y4SNxV87Wbg6K9nDQW73J","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_f810d8d75f013ad17831ce8459f07d6d:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a33c06a6-9d6e-466c-b93b-d82fa976056a","uuid":"935aea90-b0e0-42e8-a414-fbb2c3445885","timestamp":"2026-05-21T01:48:23.350Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_f810d8d75f013ad17831ce8459f07d6d:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}","structuredContent":{"result":"{\"success\": true, \"data\": {\"reviewer_key\": \"feishu:group:oc_f810d8d75f013ad17831ce8459f07d6d:reviewer_1\"}, \"error\": \"\"}"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y1YZ6nojmpfKArLr1xUSpm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GZyVtVEGHA3QicW7WZ2bLa","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Step 2: Cross-Share — Findings from R1 (Methods & Experiments)\n\nR1 has identified the following issues relevant to your novelty/positioning assessment:\n\n1. **Only 1 truly clean significant cell.** After confound analysis: GPT-4o×LLMBar is the only cell where formality OR exceeds original OR and pairwise is significant. Gemini×LLMBar has original preference OR (2.18) > formality OR (1.95), meaning the confound cannot be ruled out. This means the paper's empirical foundation is narrower than presented.\n\n2. **Regression to mean is substantial.** GPT-4o×LLMBar pairwise win rate drops from 69.1% (n=100) to 61.1% (n=500). The abstract prominently features 69.1% but the \"true\" effect is closer to 61%. The \"~7× efficiency\" claim is based on n=500 observed effects.\n\n3. **Low consistency rates (~50-56%).** Half of all pairs are discarded for position inconsistency. This creates potential selection bias and means effective sample sizes are much smaller than nominal n.\n\n4. **Binarized Likert at n=500 reaches p=0.063.** This is close to significance and suggests the \"blindspot\" is indeed more of an \"efficiency gap.\" If binarized Likert were run at n=700, it would likely be significant.\n\n5. **Likert ATE has wrong sign in some cells.** Claude×LLMBar ATE=+0.03 while pairwise shows formal preference. This directional inconsistency is unexplained.\n\n6. **Statistical methodology concerns:** Power analysis uses observed effect sizes (circular); Holm-Bonferroni applied inconsistently (within GPT-4o only, not across full 9-cell grid).\n\nPlease factor these into your novelty assessment: (a) does the narrow empirical base (1 clean cell) affect whether the \"Likert Blindspot\" concept is adequately demonstrated, (b) is the paper's contribution primarily methodological (the protocol) or empirical (the blindspot finding), and (c) how does the evidence strength affect publishability at a top venue?","reviewer_id":"2","project":"causal_style_decomp_judge"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2767,"cache_read_input_tokens":62952,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2767},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a33c06a6-9d6e-466c-b93b-d82fa976056a","uuid":"65077560-8bf4-4090-a383-9fa4d8d9fb40"}
